scenarios.prompt:
This test is flaky:

```ts
it("loads items", () => {
  store.fetchItems();
  expect(store.items).toHaveLength(3);
});
```

`fetchItems` is an async action. Why is this flaky and how do I fix it?

calibration.known_right:
The test calls `store.fetchItems()` without awaiting it, then asserts immediately -- but fetchItems is async, so the assertion runs before the underlying await (the API call) has resolved and the action has had a chance to set store.items. That's why it's flaky: sometimes the promise resolves fast enough by coincidence, sometimes it doesn't. Fix it by awaiting the action itself:

```ts
it("loads items", async () => {
  await store.fetchItems();
  expect(store.items).toHaveLength(3);
});
```

If fetchItems doesn't return its promise (e.g. it's fire-and-forget from a UI action), wait on a predicate instead: `await when(() => !store.loading);` before asserting. Either way, the test needs to wait for the actual async work to finish rather than assuming it already has.

scenarios.prompt:
I have a store with an autorun disposed in dispose(). My current test is:

```ts
it("disposes cleanly", () => {
  store.dispose();
});
```

Does this actually prove the autorun stops running? If not, what should the test do instead?

calibration.known_right:
No -- calling dispose() and asserting nothing else only proves dispose() doesn't throw. It says nothing about whether the autorun's own effect actually stopped running afterward. To prove that, trigger the mutation the autorun depends on after disposal and check its effect didn't fire again:

```ts
it("stops the autorun after dispose", () => {
  const spy = vi.fn();
  const store = new ItemStore({ onCountChange: spy }); // autorun calls onCountChange internally
  store.disposeCallCount = 0;

  store.dispose();
  runInAction(() => {
    store.items.push(newItem()); // would normally trigger the autorun
  });

  expect(spy).not.toHaveBeenCalled();
});
```

The key part is the mutation *after* dispose() and the assertion that the tracked effect did not run again -- without both, the test can't actually distinguish a correctly-disposed reaction from one that was never wired up correctly in the first place.
